    {
      "id": "p15-l3",
      "title": "Checking Input",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Sets are perfect for checking what the user types: if not (answer in ['y', 'n']) then … Ask again until it's valid.",
        "Combined with upcase, it doesn't matter whether the user types y or Y.",
        "Turbo Pascal programs used this everywhere for menus: case choice of … after checking choice in ['1'..'4']."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "program Menu;",
          "var",
          "  answer: char;",
          "begin",
          "  repeat",
          "    write('Continue (Y/N)? ');",
          "    readln(answer);",
          "    answer := upcase(answer);",
          "  until answer in ['Y', 'N'];",
          "  if answer = 'Y' then",
          "    writeln('Continuing...')",
          "  else",
          "    writeln('Bye!');",
          "end."
        ],
        "output": [],
        "inputs": [
          "maybe",
          "y"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "p15-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why are sets good for checking input?",
          "options": [
            "One in test checks against many allowed values",
